{
  "image_name": "1d69819ca49cbdce1987a12c7ccc8ee7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how autophagosomes fuse with the endolysosomal system to degrade cargo. At the top, an autophagosome can merge with an endosome to form an amphisome, and then fuse with a lysosome, or it can fuse directly with a lysosome. In both routes, the endpoint is an autolysosome, where the enclosed material appears to be broken down by lysosomal contents.\n\nThe lower schematics highlight the molecular machinery that brings the two membranes together. One layer involves SNARE proteins and tethering factors bridging the autophagosome and the late endosome/lysosome membrane. Visible SNARE combinations include STX17 and SNAP29 on the autophagosome pairing with VAMP8 or YKT6/STX7 on the opposite membrane, with ATG14 and generic tethers shown nearby.\n\nA second layer shows regulatory proteins that help organize and complete fusion. These include Rab7-associated factors, the HOPS complex, MON1–CCZ1, EPG5, TECPR1, PLEKHM1, and LC3/GABARAP-family proteins on the autophagosome surface. Lipids such as PtdIns4Ps are also indicated on the late endosome/lysosome. Overall, the image conveys that autophagosome maturation and fusion depend on coordinated tethering, Rab regulators, and SNARE-mediated membrane merger.",
